  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1046 PM MDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nA strong downstream blocking ridge will favor the trough over \nthe West to linger upstream of us through early Tuesday. This will\nkeep us in strong Southwest flow aloft and a generally warmer\nand moist environment. As mentioned earlier the highly anomalous\ntropical plume will be advecting north of the area tonight but\nstill remain above normal until the trough passes and we begin\nto dry out after midweek. Winds will remain breezy in the\nleading edge of the trough with gusts of 25 to 35 mph...with a\nfew higher gusts possible in the 4 Corners region tomorrow\nafternoon. Higher gust over the terrain can be expected as well.  \nThe parent low in this trough is over the Oregon this evening \nand model consensus has it digging through the Intermountain \nWest to the 4 Corners region by Tuesday evening. This will keep\nbroad ascent over our area supported by the jet aloft which \nwill keep working on the moisture to produce showers through\nTuesday morning. The best chances will be over the western 2/3\nof the CWA and the San Juans with less coverage over the central\ndivide. Ascent increases significantly through the day Tuesday\nas the upper low digs through the lower Great Basin. This will\nooze a cold front through the area under the strong QG signal\nsupported by the 130+kt jet aloft. This will bring another\nwidespread precipitation event through Wednesday evening. With\nthe arrival of the front and colder air aloft snow levels will\nbe dropping toward 9000 feet bringing our first hint of sweater\nweather. Though snow flakes could be falling at lower elevations\nfor a while the best accumulations are still favored to be above\n10k feet. We will continue to watch the evolution of this strong\nfall storm and hone in on finer details but widespread impacts\nto travel appear unlikely at this time. Otherwise a much cooler\nforecast will emerge through at least midweek before a slow\nwarmup begins under drier conditions on Thursday. Overnight lows\nare forecast to dip below freezing in many of the high valleys\nby Thursday as well as skies begin to clear behind this system.",
